(Walrus and April) writes:
...
>1) Wands of wishes.  I've tried wishing for scrolls of charging, but the
>wand just blows up on me when I use them.  Is there a trick to this?  Or
>are there just a limited number of wishes you can get out of a wand?

Wands of wishing can only be recharged once, so the trick is to get as
many charges out of that one recharge as you can.  The maximum number
of wishes you can get from a WoW is 7.

>2)  Statues.  I understand that some statues will have scrolls in them.  Is
>it possible to do anything else with these statues like, say, bring them to 
>life?  I know some statues do that when you step on them.  I am also
>presuming that there is a difference between a statue and a figurine.  Is
>this indeed the case?

Statues that come to life weren't really statues in the first place.
Figurines are a different beast entirely, as would be evident if you
saw two next to each other.  Figurines are tools and use the '('
symbol.  They are also a)pplied like a tool.

>3).  Dilithium Crystals.  What on EARTH do these things DO? ...

They sit in your inventory and add to your score.  One of the factors
in determining your final score is the total value of the gems you are
carrying.  Dilithium crystals are merely very valuable gems.

>4).  Naming things.  Does naming an elven dagger Sting _actually_ mean 
>that the dagger becomes Sting?  And does the same hold true for Orcrist?

Yes on both counts.  Note that there are a few limitations to this.
The item named must be of the proper type.  You could name an orcish
dagger Excalibur, but it wouldn't have any effect on its performance.
Sting and Orcrist are the only artifacts that can be created in this
manner.  Also, if Sting or Orcrist already exist somewhere else in the
game, you won't be able to create it.

>5).  Level teleport.  Okay, the description of the Amulet of Yendor says
>that you can't level teleport while it's in your possession.  Note that
>this is while you have it, not while you're wearing it.  I haven't gotten
>to the bloody thing yet,  but at least one level in my current dungeon
>doesn't have a stair down (one of the levels of Hell; I believe someone
>else here evinced a similar problem), so I'm a little concerned about the
>trek back up.  Especially if that Yendor geek is gonna be following me!:)

Only the down staircases are missing.  The up staircases will be there.

>6).  Cursed objects.  When you get the message "You hear a mumbled curse"
>do you have any way of knowing what (if anything) you own has been cursed?

This message does not mean that something in your inventory has been
cursed.  It means that the spellcaster is attempting to reduce your
luck.  There are monsters which can curse items, but the message you
would get is something like 'you notice a black aura surrounding you.'

>I believe the help files mention that once you identify something you always
>remain aware of its blessed/cursed status, but does anyone know if there's
>a way of telling with items you haven't identified yet?  (Yah, I know the
>routine:  Drop it and see if your pet walks over it/picks it up, or drop it
>on an altar; ...

My experience has been that using an altar is the most reliable method
of identifying an item's blessed/cursed status.

>7).  Holy/Unholy water.  How do you make this stuff?  ...

When you drop a potion of water on an altar, the water's blessed/
cursed status will be changed to reflect the alignment of the altar.
If you drop a potion of unholy water on a lawful altar, it will become
holy water.  Likewise, if you drop water on a chaotic altar it will
become unholy.

